Do I need a custom task manager for AI agents? 

Yes, you absolutely do! Your agents are quietly using a built-in task-manager. Now, let me convince you that making task tracking explicit and external is a good idea. You probably noticed that when you give an agent a complex task it splits it into subtasks and even shows you the task list and its current status while working on it. This task list is implicit and ephemeral - it dies when the agents feels like the task is done or when the session dies. All the context for the task and decisions made along the way vanish and you will never be able to figure out the reasoning behind the way things ended up in the codebase. This is like tracking all your work on paper and then shredding it immediately when it feels like the work item is complete. It might not be complete yet, you might figure out followups are needed, but you have already destroyed all original designs and decision notes.

Now you probably agree that you need to keep track of designs and decisions somehow, so you might think: "Alright, I can just ask my agent to maintain a documentation while working on each task". This is what most people do and it works short term, but very quickly markdown files pile up, it is not clear which ones are valid and which outdated, they pollute agent context and at some moment it starts harming more than it is helping. Many of us been there.

Some time ago I found an article from a guy who wrote a task manager that agents can use in the terminal. And about the same time I found out that all multi-agent orchestration tools have a task manager in their core. Simple task tracking is the top of the iceberg! It also acts as a structured query-able long-term memory, dependency graph tracking, as well as a communication and handover interface for agents. It is impossible to build a multi-agent workflow if your agents don't have a way to efficiently collaborate, and the way they collaborate is using a task manager - same way as we do ourselves.

