So at that moment when I saw the article I immediately got goosebumps on my back from this feeling when you know you just understood something important. I immediately downloaded it and asked my agents to use it, but was quickly disappointed because the database powering this task manager constantly got corrupted and de-synchronized, so the agent was spending lots of time just figuring out how to use it and how to recover the missing records. This thing was too complex and I had no clue why it is so complex, but I knew that it definitely shouldn't be. The author of beads did such a great job of building the features of the tool and thinking deep about agent integration, but the core foundation it was built on was just not right for my setup - it mandated specific workflow, it required too invasive surgery of the git configuration, it just didn't work for me. And you guessed it right, I started building my own task manager that is simple, intuitive, fully configurable, non-invasive and supports any workflow. But most importantly it is predictable and it just works. I have been building it for a few months already, very carefully adding only those features that are really needed and designing its architecture bit by bit. Here it is, without further adieu - Taska: https://github.com/justpresident/taska/ - AI agent task manager and workflow orchestration tool.

I studied this beads task manager from the article linked above and extracted all good ideas from it, but built them on top of very different architectural decisions that remove all the friction points I faced with the original implementation. The main idea is that whole task database can be stored in your git tree with the code as an append-only log of mutation events - the same idea high-performance databases use to absorb high rates of concurrent updates from many clients. Here it is not done for performance - it allows clean merge of branches with updates of the same task. While beads stores its task list is a separate git ref outside your code and requires configuration of custom git hooks, most in-repo task managers store task lists either as markdown files or as a single file with json strings. If two branches modify the same task - it is a merge conflict. Taska's model is simpler - it transparently installs a custom merge driver that only sees its own task log mutations file and nothing else. It cleanly merges all task updates without risking to corrupt any of your files.

