The property above is the most important one, making the tool robust. But Taska has more in it, you really need to try multiple task managers to appreciate its design! For example, all the task managers I have seen give you a set of predefined fields, like title, priority, owner and complex api with a distinct command line flag for each field, like: `-t my-task -p high -o alice`. This is a box that you have to fit in and a complex api on top of it. In taska, you define which fields your task store has. You can define a fixed schema in the config or you can live with a flexible schema. In both cases usage is intuitive: `title=my_task priority=high owner=alice`. The same syntax for task creation, mutation and filtering, no need to remember tons of flags - not for you, not for your agents.

